<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=hypocaust">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Building and engineering</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hypocaust</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 80 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Hypocaustum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A hypocaust is a system of central heating in which hot air and smoke from a furnace circulate beneath a floor raised on stacks of tiles, the pilae, and rise through clay or tile flues in the walls. Vitruvius attributes its invention to Sergius Orata around 80 BCE, and described its construction in his De architectura of about 15 BCE. Hypocausts heated public baths and the villas of the wealthy throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, and their remains survive across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. The system fell out of use in the western provinces as the empire declined, but lived on in the Eastern Roman Empire and in the baths of Al Andalus.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hypocaust.jpg" alt="Hypocaust" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pascal RADIGUE · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pilettes_et_praefurnium_maison_grand_péristyle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 80 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Italy</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Sergius Orata · <a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The earliest reference suggests that the Temple of Ephesus was heated in this way in 350 BCE, and Garrett G. Fagan has disputed Orata’s priority.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=hypocaust">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
